//! What the machine interrupts for (spec §7, §9): the scheduler tick, the
//! Timers that come due, a device line, and the idle poll.
//!
//! One comparator, two customers, composed here: a fire says only that
//! something may be due, so every customer is asked out of one reading of the
//! clock and the tick is asked first.
//!
//! Everything here services state that lives below it (the timer slabs in
//! `time`, the interrupt slab in `waitables`, the ready queue in `threads`) and
//! tells somebody through `refs`. The scheduler above it acts on the flag a
//! tick raises.

import hal
import kcore.limits.{MAX_INTERRUPTS, MAX_TIMERS, NO_INTERRUPT, NO_THREAD}
import kcore.diag.{console}
import kcore.threads.{
    CURRENT_THREAD,
    NEED_RESCHED,
    READY_HEAD,
    SLICE_LEFT,
    SLICE_TICKS,
}
import kcore.time.{NEXT_TICK_NS, TICK_PERIOD_NS, TIMERS, reprogram_timer}
import kcore.waitables.{INTERRUPTS, WaitableKind, interrupt_by_line}
import kcore.refs.{notify_ready}

/// Fire every Timer whose deadline `now` has reached.
///
/// No preemption point, by definition: this walk is the IRQ path, so
/// `IN_DELIVERY` is set for the whole of it and a point written here would be
/// inert anyway. The same holds for every loop it reaches (the wake walks and
/// the attachment unlinks under `notify_ready`). The bound is the Timer slab's
/// `capacity()`, of O(1) work per slot.
func expire_timers(now: UInt64) unsafe {
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < TIMERS.capacity() {
        match TIMERS[i].state {
            case Live -> {
                if TIMERS[i].is_due(now) {
                    fire_timer(i, now)
                }
            },
            case Free -> {},
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
}

/// One Timer came due: advance its schedule, and tell its Waiter.
///
/// `TimerSlot.fire` is the whole of the drift-free re-arm and the coalescing
/// division, arithmetic on one slot's own fields and so a safe function. What
/// is left here is the half that reaches the rest of the kernel: the slab index
/// and the wake.
func fire_timer(slot: Int, now: UInt64) unsafe {
    TIMERS[slot].fire(now)
    notify_ready(WaitableKind.Timer, slot)
}

// =============================================================================
// Interrupts (spec §9)
// =============================================================================
//
// The hardware never delivers an interrupt in kernel mode; the kernel asks, at
// named points.
//
// First, the machines enforce it. An interrupt is taken from user mode and
// nowhere else: the kernel runs with them masked from boot, each machine masks
// them again on trap entry in hardware, and each machine's return to user mode
// is where they come back on. Nothing below arranges any of that; each HAL
// states in its ABI.md that an interrupt arriving in kernel mode lands on its
// kernel-bug path rather than on this code. So there are no critical sections
// here, no interrupt masking vocabulary, and no `IntrSpinLock` (spec §9b) until
// SMP needs one.
//
// Second, a long kernel operation polls. At a `preempt_point()` (below, beside
// the idle poll it is modelled on) the kernel asks the controller what is
// pending and services it synchronously through the same `deliver_line` the
// trap path uses. From the interrupt's side the semantics are the trap's:
// serviced at the point, the operation resumes. It is not a brief unmask, so
// the hardware enforcement above stays a live tripwire rather than becoming a
// delivery path.
//
// The placement rule for a point: it is legal where no in-flight invariant
// spans state the IRQ path touches. `IN_DELIVERY` below is its mechanical
// backstop for the code the kernel's two contexts share.
//
// Arch-free here: which hook a line belongs to, what the kernel does with it,
// and the order of claim / service / complete. Seam calls: how a line is
// claimed, masked and completed, and how a timer is rearmed. A point adds no
// seam; it is `irq_poll` again, which the idle path already needs.

/// How many of the first ticks the kernel narrates on the console.
///
/// Logging every tick would make the log the workload (at a millisecond
/// period), so the hook says enough to prove the path ran and then goes quiet.
/// The count keeps rising either way.
static TICK_REPORT_LIMIT: UInt = 4

/// Ticks the periodic timer has delivered since boot.
///
/// Compound global state resting on a serialization argument the compiler
/// cannot see (`unsafe static var`): uniprocessor kernel, interrupts taken from
/// user mode only, so the tick path is the only writer and nothing can
/// interleave with it.
///
/// Both counters below wrap on purpose (`&+`, not `+`). Overflow checks are
/// always on, so a plain `+` would panic on a full counter, and a kernel that
/// stops after weeks of uptime or four billion spurious interrupts has turned a
/// number nobody was reading into an outage. Deltas across a wrap are still
/// right.
unsafe static var TIMER_TICKS: UInt = 0

/// Interrupts the controller had nothing to give for. One is not an error (a
/// line can stop asserting between raising the interrupt and being asked about
/// it), but a stream of them is, so the first is reported and the rest are
/// counted.
unsafe static var SPURIOUS_IRQS: UInt = 0

/// Is a delivery in flight? The reentrancy guard for the code the kernel's two
/// contexts share.
///
/// The byte movers serve both. The loader's segment placement is preemptible
/// boot context and copies whole segments; `copy_out` runs in IRQ context
/// (`refs.deliver_attachment` writes a parked thread's wait record from the
/// waker's context, and for an expired Timer that context is under
/// `deliver_line`). A preemption point inside a mover must therefore be inert
/// when the mover runs under delivery, or a timer coming due during that copy
/// would re-enter delivery mid-delivery.
///
/// So the flag is set around `deliver_line`'s whole body and checked first in
/// `preempt_point`. It converts a placement error in shared code from a
/// corruption into a no-op at one auditable site, rather than a convention
/// every call site must remember. Every way into delivery funnels through
/// `deliver_line` (the trap path's `service_irq`, the idle poll, the preemption
/// point), so one flag covers all three.
///
/// The copies the IRQ path makes are bounded records (`WaitBuffer`, whose body
/// is at most `PIPE_BODY_BYTES`), far under one stride, so the guard is a
/// backstop for them; it is what would make a longer mover safe.
///
/// Compound global state resting on a serialization argument the compiler
/// cannot see (`unsafe static var`): uniprocessor kernel, hardware delivery
/// from user mode only, and the polled entries are this flag's own readers, so
/// the single kernel context is the only writer and no interleaving between the
/// set and the clear is expressible. Under SMP the flag becomes per-core state
/// behind the IntrSpinLock spec §9b reserves.
public(package) unsafe static var IN_DELIVERY: Bool = false

/// Ticks the periodic timer has delivered since boot. The scheduler's clock
/// when there is a scheduler; otherwise the harness's evidence that a tick was
/// taken, and when.
public func timer_ticks() unsafe -> UInt {
    TIMER_TICKS
}

/// The timer interrupt: the arch-free landing point for the one comparator.
///
/// A fire says only that something may be due, so every customer is asked out
/// of one reading of the clock, and the tick is asked first (a timeslice charge
/// that a Timer's servicing delayed would be a scheduler bug in a Timer's
/// clothes). Using one `now` for both means the tick and every Timer are judged
/// against the same instant: a Timer cannot be late because the tick ran first,
/// and the tick cannot slip because several Timers came due together.
/// Programming the next deadline is last, and it also lowers the line (both
/// machines' timer condition is a level).
///
/// `pc` is where the interrupted context resumes, or one of the two poll
/// sentinels when nothing trapped: `IDLE_PC` when the idle path found the fire,
/// `PREEMPT_PC` when a preemption point did.
func on_timer_interrupt(pc: UInt) unsafe {
    let now = hal.timer_now_ns()

    if TICK_PERIOD_NS != 0 && now >= NEXT_TICK_NS {
        // Measured from now rather than from the deadline missed: a tick the
        // kernel was late for is one tick, never a burst of catch-up ticks
        // whose only effect would be to keep the machine in the trap handler. A
        // Timer's rule is the opposite (see `fire_timer`): a timeslice has no
        // history to be faithful to, and an interval does.
        NEXT_TICK_NS = now + TICK_PERIOD_NS
        on_timer_tick(pc)
    }

    expire_timers(now)
    reprogram_timer()
}

/// The timer tick: the arch-free landing point for the periodic timer.
///
/// The timeslice is charged here and nothing else about scheduling is: the tick
/// decrements the running thread's slice and, when it runs out, raises a flag.
/// It does not switch, walk the ready queue or touch the frame. The tick runs
/// with interrupts masked, so its length is interrupt latency, while the switch
/// it motivates happens later at the user-return boundary.
///
/// `pc` is where the interrupted context will resume, read out of the frame by
/// the caller, or a poll sentinel when the tick was found rather than taken:
/// `IDLE_PC` from the idle path, `PREEMPT_PC` from a preemption point. The
/// report prints whichever, so a transcript says which of the three deliveries
/// ran.
func on_timer_tick(pc: UInt) unsafe {
    TIMER_TICKS = TIMER_TICKS &+ 1

    if CURRENT_THREAD != NO_THREAD {
        if SLICE_LEFT > 0 {
            SLICE_LEFT = SLICE_LEFT - 1
        }
        if SLICE_LEFT == 0 {
            NEED_RESCHED = true
            SLICE_LEFT = SLICE_TICKS
        }
    }

    if TIMER_TICKS > TICK_REPORT_LIMIT {
        return
    }
    let uart = console()
    uart.write_str("SOS: timer tick ")
    uart.write_hex(TIMER_TICKS)
    uart.write_str(" at ")
    uart.write_hex(pc)
    uart.write_str("\n")
}

/// What the tick report prints for a tick taken with nothing running. The
/// kernel was idle, so there is no interrupted address, and zero is the honest
/// answer rather than a stale one.
static IDLE_PC: UInt = 0

/// A device interrupt: the arch-free landing point for an external line, and
/// spec §9's delivery.
///
/// The line is already masked when this runs (`deliver_line` below), so what is
/// left is to mark the object ready and tell its Waiter. Marking before waking
/// makes the level-triggered path work in both orders: a driver already parked
/// is woken with the record, and one that has not waited yet finds `pending`
/// set by its own next `Wait`.
///
/// A line nothing is bound to is reported and left masked, the only safe
/// answer: the kernel cannot service a device it has no driver for, and an
/// unmasked line with nobody to ack it would re-fire forever. This is also the
/// path the interrupt-controller bring-up case exercises, which keeps
/// `irq_raise_selftest_line` meaningful with no Interrupt object involved.
func on_external_irq(line: UInt) unsafe {
    let irq = interrupt_by_line(line)
    if irq == NO_INTERRUPT {
        let uart = console()
        uart.write_str("SOS: external irq ")
        uart.write_hex(line)
        uart.write_str("\n")
        return
    }
    INTERRUPTS[irq].pending = true
    notify_ready(WaitableKind.Interrupt, irq)
}

/// Service one claimed line: the arch-free half of an interrupt, and the one
/// place a line number turns into work.
///
/// Its three entry points have all already claimed the line from the
/// controller:
///
///   - `service_irq`, from the trap path, with the interrupted PC;
///   - `idle_poll`, from `pick_next`'s idle loop, with `IDLE_PC`;
///   - `preempt_point`, from inside a long operation, with `PREEMPT_PC`.
///
/// The guard wraps the whole body because what it asserts, "a delivery is in
/// flight", is true from the claim to the completion. The body is a function of
/// its own because Saw has no `defer`: a body with an early return would need
/// the clear written twice, and the second one is the one a later edit forgets.
func deliver_line(line: UInt, pc: UInt) unsafe {
    IN_DELIVERY = true
    service_claimed_line(line, pc)
    IN_DELIVERY = false
}

/// The delivery itself, under the guard above and reached through nothing else.
///
/// Its two branches differ in exactly one step (spec §9): the timer programs
/// its next deadline, which lowers its line, while a device line is masked,
/// because a device goes on asserting until its driver has been told.
func service_claimed_line(line: UInt, pc: UInt) unsafe {
    if line == hal.IRQ_NONE {
        SPURIOUS_IRQS = SPURIOUS_IRQS &+ 1
        if SPURIOUS_IRQS == 1 {
            console().write_str("SOS: spurious interrupt\n")
        }
        return
    }

    if line == hal.IRQ_TIMER {
        on_timer_interrupt(pc)
    } else {
        hal.irq_mask(line)
        on_external_irq(line)
    }
    hal.irq_complete(line)
}

/// Every interrupt the kernel takes arrives here, through `ktrap` and nothing
/// else. (Interrupts the kernel polls for arrive through `idle_poll` while it
/// has nothing to run and through `preempt_point` during a long operation: the
/// same service over two other ways of noticing.)
public(package) func service_irq(frame_addr: UInt, cause: UInt) unsafe {
    deliver_line(hal.irq_claim(cause), hal.trap_pc(frame_addr))
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The idle path (spec §7)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// "Nothing runnable" is a deadlock unless readiness can arrive from outside the
// set of runnable threads. An Event is signalled by some thread, so with none
// runnable nobody is left to signal one. A bound IRQ line (or an armed timer)
// is readiness from outside that set, so the same state has two readings and
// the kernel has to tell them apart.
//
// The rule: idle if any Interrupt is bound or any Timer is armed, deadlock
// otherwise. It is deliberately conservative: the kernel does not try to prove
// a specific blocked thread is reachable by a specific line, which would mean
// walking attachment sets to answer a question that a wrong answer only makes
// slower to diagnose (a hang at the harness timeout instead of a report). What
// it never does is call a real wait a deadlock.
//
// The idle loop polls instead of taking the interrupt. Interrupts are taken
// from user mode only, enforced by the machines, so an interrupt arriving while
// the kernel idles is pending rather than delivered. `wait_for_irq` parks the
// core until one is pending (both machines wake from it on a pending interrupt
// whether or not it is deliverable), and `irq_poll` then asks the controller
// for it directly. The kernel still never takes a trap in kernel mode, and the
// same `deliver_line` services the line either way.
/// Ask the controller for a line, and service it if there is one.
func idle_poll() unsafe {
    let line = hal.irq_poll()
    if line == hal.IRQ_NONE {
        return
    }
    deliver_line(line, IDLE_PC)
}

/// Is there a source of readiness outside the set of runnable threads?
///
/// Two kinds answer yes. A bound IRQ line is readiness a device will deliver;
/// an armed timer is readiness the clock will deliver. Neither needs a thread
/// to run for it to arrive.
///
/// An armed timer counts and a merely existing one does not: a disarmed Timer
/// will never fire, so a system holding only those is as deadlocked as one
/// holding none. Counting a sleeping process (one that armed a timer and
/// parked) as a deadlock would terminate exactly the program that works; the
/// other error turns a real deadlock into a harness timeout.
///
/// A supervisor attached to a Process adds no arm. A process death arrives from
/// inside the thread set (some syscall or fault of that process caused it, so
/// one of its threads had to run), which is the negation of what this predicate
/// asks. The ways the question can come up all resolve without one:
///
///   - parked on a live, started child: that child's threads are runnable, so
///     the ready queue is not empty and this predicate is never consulted;
///   - parked on a dead child: `waiter_wait`'s scan found `Gone` and returned
///     without parking (terminal level), so this case does not exist;
///   - parked on a child that was created and never started: it has no threads
///     and never will, so nothing can ever make it `Gone`. That is a genuine
///     deadlock, and answering `false` here is correct. Don't add an arm
///     counting live processes: it turns exactly this mistake into a silent
///     hang.
public(package) func has_external_wake_source() unsafe -> Bool {
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < INTERRUPTS.capacity() {
        match INTERRUPTS[i].state {
            case Live -> { return true },
            case Free -> {},
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    var t: Int = 0
    while t < TIMERS.capacity() {
        match TIMERS[t].state {
            case Live -> {
                if TIMERS[t].armed {
                    return true
                }
            },
            case Free -> {},
        }
        t = t + 1
    }
    false
}

/// Park the core until something is runnable again.
///
/// The exit condition is the ready queue, not the poll: a serviced line wakes a
/// waiter through `deliver_line` -> `on_external_irq` -> `notify_ready`, which
/// pushes that thread onto the queue. A line that wakes nobody (one bound to an
/// Interrupt no thread is waiting on) leaves the queue empty and the loop goes
/// back to sleep, which is why the test is on the queue rather than on "an
/// interrupt happened".
///
/// No preemption point: this loop is delivery, and the bounded scans beside it
/// (`has_external_wake_source`) are `MAX_INTERRUPTS` + `MAX_TIMERS` iterations
/// of O(1) work.
public(package) func idle_until_runnable() unsafe {
    while READY_HEAD == 0 {
        hal.wait_for_irq()
        idle_poll()
    }
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The preemption point
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// A point is the idle poll with a different reason for asking. The idle path
// polls because the kernel has nothing to run; a long operation polls because
// the kernel has been running too long. Both ask the same seam, service through
// the same funnel, and leave the hardware rule (interrupts are taken from user
// mode only) where it was. Briefly unmasking instead would need a kernel-mode
// trap path on both machines and would spend the tripwire that catches a kernel
// bug.
//
// A point interrupts latency, not atomicity. A reschedule the delivery signals
// (`NEED_RESCHED` from a tick, a wake from an expired Timer) is honored at the
// user-return boundary through `pick_next`; the operation runs to completion
// before the switch it may have motivated.
//
// The points themselves are in `kcore.preempt`, one module above this one,
// which holds the strided long-op movers that call this. See that module's
// header for why they are not here or in `mem`.

/// The sentinel a tick report prints for a tick taken at a preemption point.
///
/// Distinct from `IDLE_PC` so a transcript can tell "the kernel was idle" from
/// "the kernel was halfway through a long copy". Like zero it can never
/// collide with a real interrupted PC: an odd address is not an instruction
/// address, since both machines fetch on at least a two-byte boundary.
static PREEMPT_PC: UInt = 1

/// A preemption point: the kernel asking, mid-operation, whether the machine
/// wants anything.
///
/// The guard is first, and it is not an optimization: `IN_DELIVERY` set means
/// this call is already inside the IRQ path, where a second delivery would
/// re-enter the tables the first one is halfway through. Returning is the whole
/// handling; the interrupt stays pending and is taken at the next legal moment.
///
/// The caller owes the placement sentence. This function cannot check that no
/// in-flight invariant spans state the IRQ path touches; that is a property of
/// where the call is written, so every call site carries a one-line verdict.
public(package) func preempt_point() unsafe {
    if IN_DELIVERY {
        return
    }
    let line = hal.irq_poll()
    if line == hal.IRQ_NONE {
        return
    }
    deliver_line(line, PREEMPT_PC)
}
